<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- 🔴 AF-1 (.superpowers/sdd/item12-stage3/task-1-brief.md) — ITEM 12 OF docs/owner-decisions.md,
         ENFORCED, STAGE 3 OF 8. The ruling is: turn this switch on here, WITH NO EXEMPTION OF ANY KIND,
         and let the debt be NAMED rather than silenced.

         🔴 IT IS NOT ONE OF THE FOUR LABELLED OPTIONS, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT WAS.
         That draft wrote "the owner ruled option (b)". The decision itself warns, in the sentence
         immediately above it, that copying a label into an implementation site is how the ruling gets
         enforced BACKWARDS: (a) is an assembly-wide `<NoWarn>`, i.e. MAXIMUM silence, the exact opposite
         of what was decided; (b1) and (b2) are overrides too, by the decision's own words; (c) is not
         turning the switch on at all. The ruling is a FIFTH thing that none of the four labels names, and
         it is recorded BY DESCRIPTION, never by label. Corrected here rather than quietly rewritten,
         because a wrong label at the enforcement site is worth more than a wrong number.

         WHAT THIS ONE PROPERTY COSTS, MEASURED on a full `dotnet build -t:Rebuild` of this solution at
         the commit that adds it — never derived by arithmetic, because stage 1 measured that the number
         is NOT preserved under payment (writing `<param>` for SOME parameters of a member converts one
         CS1591 into one CS1573, so a subtraction would understate):
           116 -> 852 warnings across the tree, all of them in this compilation (+736), 0 errors.
           736 = 543 CS1591 + 92 CS1573 + 75 CS1574 + 23 CS1734 + 3 CS0419.
         Of those 736, 103 (95 CS1591 + 8 CS1573) fall in the vendored SDK file this project
         `Compile Include`s — examples/device-client/csharp/St4iDeviceClient.cs — which this repository
         MAY NOT EDIT. They are the named debt. They must remain VISIBLE; silencing them is an owner
         decision and lowering a pin to hide them is how a named debt stops being named.

         🔴 THE DEBT IS NOT PAID BY THIS COMMIT AND ITEM 12 STAYS IN PART II BECAUSE OF IT. 633 of the
         736 are ours to pay (101 unresolvable `cref`/`paramref` claims — assertions that are already
         FALSE — plus 532 coverage gaps across 90 files) and stages 4..8 pay them, each re-MEASURING its
         own constant. The two instruments that keep this honest in the meantime are the origin-split
         ledger beside EXPECT_WARNINGS in scripts/verify-suites.sh (which pins 103 vendored + 633 ours
         by (bucket, code), both directions) and SuppressionCensusTests, which pins THIS row: the census
         went red on this edit before its table was moved, which is the evidence that it watches the
         switch rather than the warnings. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\..\..\..\examples\device-client\csharp\St4iDeviceClient.cs" Link="Sdk\St4iDeviceClient.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- GP-1 — the driver contract (IDeviceDriver, DeviceReading, CyclePlan, TelemetryNumeric, and the
         contract enums) now lives in this plain-net10.0, zero-dependency project so a third party can
         compile a driver against it without pulling in EdgeCore's Windows-only DPAPI/vendored-SDK
         surface. See task-1-report.md for the full rationale. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\St4i.Connector.Abstractions\St4i.Connector.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- GĐ3 closeout WI-1 (.superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-giaidoan3-ws-i-closeout-blueprint/task-1-brief.md)
         Part A — the Makaretu.Dns.Multicast.New PackageReference that SD-1 originally added here MOVED to
         St4i.EngineApi.csproj: Site/SiteDiscovery.cs (the only consumer) moved there with it, and
         St4i.EngineApi is EngineApi's only consumer of ISiteDiscovery (SiteEndpoints.DiscoverAsync).
         St4i.EdgeService/St4iMachineSimulator (the WPF exhibition app) both ONLY ProjectReference this
         project, never EngineApi, and never touch mDNS at all — so they were carrying Makaretu's (and its
         transitive Common.Logging's) DLLs in their own build output for no reason (NuGet's
         ProjectReference-transitive-package-copy propagates a package to every downstream consumer's
         output regardless of whether that consumer's own code uses it). See
         St4i.EdgeCore.Tests.MakaretuNotShippedTests for the proof those two outputs are now clean, and the
         task-1-report.md for the full rationale. -->
    <!-- 🔴 WS-E (License/Edition) — the ONE new NuGet this workstream adds, and it enters the TRUST ROOT
         of the revenue mechanism, so the reason is recorded here rather than inferred from the using.

         WHY A PACKAGE AT ALL, MEASURED not assumed: .NET 10's System.Security.Cryptography (10.0.0.0)
         exports MLDsa/SlhDsa/CompositeMLDsa/ECDsa and their Cng/OpenSsl variants and has NO Ed25519 and
         NO EdDsa type — the post-quantum suite shipped and EdDSA was skipped. The roadmap names Ed25519
         (roadmap:136, :305, :342) and the owner ruled on 2026-09-06 to keep the spec's letter rather than
         substitute ECDSA P-256, which the platform does have. Verified before this reference was added:
         BouncyCastle 2.7.0 signs, verifies, produces a 32-byte public key and a 64-byte signature, and
         REJECTS a tampered payload — the last of which is the property that matters and the one a
         verifier that accepts everything would also pass a happy-path test for.

         🔴 NO PRIVATE KEY EVER ENTERS THIS REPOSITORY. Only the 32-byte public key is embedded
         (LicenseVerifier.EmbeddedPublicKey). Test licences are signed by a key pair generated IN MEMORY
         inside the test fixture and verified through LicenseVerifier's SPKI-injection seam; nothing
         writes a private key to the tree, and LicensePrivateKeyAbsenceTests parses the source to prove
         no key-generation call site exists in src/. -->
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.7.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="MQTTnet" Version="5.2.0.1603" />
    <PackageReference Include="MQTTnet.Server" Version="5.2.0.1603" />
    <!-- G2-2 (UNS spine) — the ONE new NuGet this task adds: runtime-only, used by
         Uns/Sparkplug/SparkplugPayload.cs's hand-rolled Sparkplug B encoder/decoder (CodedOutputStream/
         CodedInputStream low-level tag/value primitives), deliberately WITHOUT protoc/Grpc.Tools — no
         .proto files, no generated code, just the wire-format primitives this runtime package ships. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.35.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- FF-2 / NU1903: Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.10 pins the transitive SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3
         (and, through it, SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3) at 2.1.11, which the NuGet audit flags via
         GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q / CVE-2025-6965 (bundled SQLite < 3.50.2, a memory-corruption issue).
         2.1.12 is the first patched release (confirmed: a scratch project pinned to this version
         reports `select sqlite_version()` = 3.53.3, well past the 3.50.2 fix line, and a fresh
         `dotnet restore` against it emits no NU1903). Pinning it explicitly here — NuGet's
         nearest-wins resolution picks this direct reference over the 2.1.11 transitive minimum
         Microsoft.Data.Sqlite declares — clears the advisory without touching Microsoft.Data.Sqlite
         itself. See README "NU1903" note for the full rationale. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData" Version="9.0.0" />
    <!-- G2-6 (Modbus TCP driver) — the ONE new NuGet this task adds: NModbus 3.0.83, the maintained
         `rich.quackenbush` fork (NOT the abandoned NModbus4) — provides the TCP master
         (Drivers/Modbus/ModbusTcpDriver.cs) and, test-only, the in-process TCP slave/slave-network the
         loopback test stands up (no real hardware). MIT-licensed. -->
    <PackageReference Include="NModbus" Version="3.0.83" />
    <!-- GĐ3 sub-3 OU-1 (OPC-UA driver) — the ONE new NuGet this task adds: the OPC Foundation .NET
         reference-stack CLIENT library, relicensed GPLv2/RCL -> MIT on 2025-12-04 (see the licensing
         spike write-up in docs/plans/2026-07-27-giaidoan3-opcua-driver-blueprint.md). Pinned EXACT
         (never a floating range) — the relicense is recent, so drifting below this exact MIT release
         would silently re-expose the old GPLv2/RCL terms. -->
    <PackageReference Include="OPCFoundation.NetStandard.Opc.Ua.Client" Version="1.5.378.156" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
